Shanling Q2 tools
=================

The Q2 runs Linux 5.10 on an Ingenic X2000 (MIPS32r2, nan2008, FP64) with
glibc 2.28, no IPv6 and no CA bundle. librespot runs from the card's .spotify
folder as a Spotify Connect receiver beside the stock player, and Q2 Pod
(https://github.com/DiamondBond/q2-pod) starts it, shows what it plays on
Streaming -> Spotify, and hands it the keys, the DAC and the power timers.
Nothing is flashed for it.

  build.sh     hard-float build against the Q2's own glibc, and the card files,
               in target/shanlingq2/.spotify (copy that folder to the card's root)
  launcher.sh  installed as .spotify/run: librespot in a restart loop, in the
               background, once per boot
  aplay.sh     the subprocess sink: the stock aplay on plughw:0,0, retried for
               3 s while local music lets the PCM go; Q2 Pod's q2video -s on
               the device /tmp/q2-librespot.out names (Bluetooth, USB DAC)
  mipsel-q2-linux-gnu.json
               the Rust target: MIPS32r2, FP64, NaN-2008, glibc
  q2-cc, q2-link
               the target's C compiler (ring) and linker, clang and lld
  start.c, nonshared.c
               what the rootfs lacks to link against: crt1.o's __start and
               libc_nonshared.a's stat64 family and pthread_atfork

Install
-------

Releases ship the card files prebuilt: unzip q2-librespot-*.zip from
https://github.com/DiamondBond/q2-librespot/releases/latest to the card's
root with the Q2 powered off. See the top-level README.md.

Build
-----

Rust nightly with rust-src (std is built with -Zbuild-std for the custom
target), clang, lld, llvm-ar and llvm-strip 18 or later, unsquashfs, python3,
MIPS glibc headers for ring's C code, and Shanling's Q2 V1.32 firmware ZIP,
whose rootfs supplies the libraries the binary links against:

  rustup toolchain install nightly --profile minimal --component rust-src
  sudo apt install clang lld llvm squashfs-tools libc6-dev-mipsel-cross
  LLVM_SUFFIX=-18 contrib/shanlingq2/build.sh 'Q2 Firmware V1.32.zip'

(LLVM_SUFFIX only where the LLVM tools carry their version, as on Ubuntu.)

The binary is hard float for the X2000's FPU: FP64 (FR=1) and NaN-2008, the
ABI of the stock binaries, dynamically linked against the Q2's glibc 2.28
through /lib/ld-linux-mipsn8.so.1, as Q2 Pod's own helpers are. Rust has no
such target: mipsel-q2-linux-gnu.json is mipsel-unknown-linux-gnu with its
+fpxx,+nooddspreg (and legacy NaN) replaced by +fp64,+nan2008,+abs2008, PIE off,
and q2-link as a gnu-cc linker. The rootfs has no crt1.o or libc_nonshared.a:
q2-link puts start.o in crt1.o's place, and build.sh writes glibc's usual
libc.so linker script around libc.so.6 and nonshared.c's archive. rustc marks
its own symbols.o NaN-2008 only for r6 CPUs, so q2-link sets the flag on it (it
holds no float code). Cargo does not see start.o, the archive or q2-link: after
editing them, delete target/mipsel-q2-linux-gnu so librespot is linked again.
librespot-core enables num-traits' std feature, so the TPDF dither's square
root is the FPU's sqrt.d. TLS is rustls with webpki's compiled-in roots.

Measured with a harness running librespot's decode chain (Symphonia Vorbis into
f64, TPDF dither, S16) built both ways with a fixed dither seed: the hard-float
PCM is bit-identical to the soft-float PCM (on the Q2 and in QEMU), and 30 s of
Vorbis took 9.4 s of CPU soft-float and 0.86 s hard-float on the Q2 at
160 kbit/s (9.5 and 0.92 s at 320), so decoding drops from about 31% to 3% of a
core. Whole-process CPU during real Spotify playback is not measured yet.

Device
------

Discovery binds 0.0.0.0 when [::] fails (this branch's discovery fix). Q2 Pod
runs .spotify/run when Streaming's Spotify row is first pressed after each
boot, and never otherwise; .spotify/cache keeps the login (the first Connect
handoff saves it).

librespot is started with:

  --backend subprocess --device "/bin/sh ./aplay.sh"
      aplay runs only while librespot plays, so the PCM is free for local
      music whenever it is paused (hciplayer takes one client per PCM)
  --volume-ctrl fixed --initial-volume 100
      the Q2's volume is the DAC's, which Q2 Pod sets, or on Bluetooth and
      a USB DAC without its own volume q2video's soft volume
  --status-file /tmp/q2-librespot.state
      this branch: the state (none without a Connect session, stopped,
      paused, playing), the track's id, title, artists, album and duration,
      its position at a CLOCK_MONOTONIC time, and cover=1 once the file's
      .jpg holds the track's cover, which librespot downloads itself
  --control-socket /tmp/q2-librespot.sock
      this branch: datagrams 'p' play/pause, 'P' play, 's' pause,
      'n' next, 'b' previous, 'S' and ms to seek, from the Q2's keys
  --browse-socket /tmp/q2-librespot.browse.sock
      this branch (q2-v1.6): Q2 Pod's Spotify browser, a stream socket
      with one request and one response per connection (Q2SPOT/1:
      playlists, Liked Songs, albums, artists, search, play, queue,
      shuffle, repeat, downloaded tracks, playlist downloads, sign out); the protocol is in
      q2-pod's docs/librespot.md. Complete audio files are capped at 2 GB, or
      .spotify/.cache-size (Q2 Pod's Streaming > Configuration),
      and saved track metadata, keys and artwork allow offline replay.
      Sign out deletes the login, cached audio, keys and artwork.
      Q2SPOT/1 download <playlist-uri> starts one background playlist download;
      downloadstatus returns its progress in the response message. Existing
      complete tracks are reused. The normal cache cap still applies, so a
      playlist larger than the cap can evict its earlier tracks.
      /tmp/q2-librespot.download reports downloading=1 while the job runs,
      independently of playback, so Q2 Pod keeps the service awake.
      A saved login opens the offline browser even with no downloaded tracks

Logs go to the card: spot.log, aplay.log. With a file named debug in
.spotify, load.log gets the load average and librespot's VmRSS every 10 s,
and Q2 Pod writes q2pod.log (each hand-over step).

Power the Q2 off before taking the card out or putting it in: swapping it
while the Q2 was on or asleep corrupted the FAT twice.

Status
------

Works on the device: discovery, a Connect session from the phone and audio
on the headphones at 160 kbit/s, with no stutter reported. The hard-float
build (q2-v1.7) was tested on the Q2 with Spotify playback before release. Q2 Pod's integration (the page, keys, volume, standby, DAC and the
hand-over with local music) is described in q2-pod's docs/internals.md
(Spotify). With Bluetooth or a USB DAC as the Q2's output, Q2 Pod writes
/tmp/q2-librespot.out ("plug:bluealsa 42": the ALSA device and the volume,
"h" before it when a USB DAC applies it) and aplay.sh plays through Q2 Pod's
/usr/bin/q2video -s DEVICE VOLUME, which applies hciplayer's soft volume and
takes volume changes from Q2 Pod on /tmp/q2sink.sock. Without the file, as
with older Q2 Pod, it is aplay on the headphone DAC. The output is picked at
each sink start: one switched while playing takes over after pause and play.
